  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Neoerysiphe chelones is a powdery mildew fungus that infects various plant species throughout New England, causing a characteristic white, powdery coating on leaves and stems. This obligate parasite relies entirely on living plant tissue to survive and reproduce.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "White Powdery Coating",
      "description": "Distinctive white to grayish fungal growth appears on leaf surfaces, stems, and occasionally flowers",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Chasmothecia Formation",
      "description": "Dark, spherical fruiting bodies (cleistothecia) develop within the white mycelium as the season progresses",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Host Plant Specificity",
      "description": "Primarily found on plants in the Chelone genus and related species in the Plantaginaceae family",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Neoerysiphe chelones thrives in cool, moist environments typical of New England forests and gardens, particularly on shade-tolerant herbaceous plants and native wildflowers.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike many powdery mildews, Neoerysiphe chelones produces overwintering spores that allow it to survive harsh New England winters and emerge in spring to infect new plant growth.",
